<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tydings–McDuffie Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Philippine Independence Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tydings–McDuffie Act, or Philippine Independence Act, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> law of 1934 providing for Philippine independence after a ten-year transition. It created the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, with its own constitution and elected president, in 1935, while the United States kept control of defence and foreign affairs. Independence followed on 4 July 1946.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A first attempt to fix a date for independence, the Hare–Hawes–Cutting Act of 1933, was passed by Congress over President Hoover's veto but needed the consent of the Philippine Senate. Its President, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel L. Quezon</a>, persuaded the senators to reject it because it would have let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> keep military bases in the islands permanently. In 1934 Quezon led a Philippine Independence mission to Washington, which lobbied Congress for a new bill. Written by Senator Millard Tydings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and Representative John McDuffie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, it was signed by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> on 24 March 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The act set a procedure for drafting a constitution of the Commonwealth within two years, laid down provisions it had to contain, and required its approval by the US president and by Filipinos. Until independence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> could keep military forces in the islands and call all Philippine forces into its service, and within two years after independence the president could negotiate over naval reservations and fuelling stations. The act also reclassified all Filipinos, including those living in the United States, as aliens for immigration purposes, with a quota of 50 immigrants a year; until then they had been US nationals, free to migrate but denied naturalisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Immigration in fact ran well above the quota, as agricultural lobbies such as the Hawaiian sugar planters won leave to bring in more male Filipino farm workers. The act extended the Asian-exclusion policy of the Immigration Act of 1924 to the islands, so that a Filipino in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> who went home on a visit might never be allowed back, and it led to the Filipino Repatriation Act. The Luce–Celler Act of 1946 raised the quota to 100 a year and opened naturalisation to Filipinos. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a>'s Proclamation 2695 of 4 July 1946 recognised the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tydings–McDuffie Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tydings-McDuffie Act</b> of 1934 provided for a ten-year transition to independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>Campaigns by Manuel Quezon and Sergio Osmena led to the <b>Tydings-McDuffie Act</b> of 1934, and in November 1935 the Insular Government was replaced by the Commonwealth of the Philippines as a transition to independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</a></li></ul></section>
